  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nOn Sunday, the upper level ridge over the west and associated \nupper high slide slightly eastward, with the aforementioned \nupper trough continuing to pivot over the eastern CONUS keeping \nus in northwesterly flow aloft. During the day, conditions look \nmainly dry, with highs topping out in the 80s to even the low \n90s for southeast KS and far southwest MO. Another upper level \ndisturbance looks to slide southeast into the area again Sunday \nnight, leading to additional chances (50-60%) for showers and \nstorms. CAMs show two clusters of showers/storms developing: One\npushing southeast from the Dakotas into northern MO, and a \nsecond developing over KS/OK pushing east. As these push through\nthe area overnight and into Monday morning, they could pose a \nlocalized flooding threat, as some guidance suggests PWATS over \n2.0\". As the previous discussion mentioned, this would be \n150-175% above normal. Current HREF 24-hr LPMMs show localized \npockets of 1.5-2.5\" of rainfall, with the majority of the area \nexpected to remain below an inch. There's still much uncertainty\nregarding this activity, with several key mesoscale factors \nthat need to come together to form any hydro concerns. \n\nWith the area remaining in northwesterly flow throughout the\nweek, we'll see periodic low-end chances (15-30%) for \nshowers/storms. Daily highs should generally remain in the upper\n80s to low 90s (northeast CWA) to the mid/upper 90s (southwest \nCWA).",
